\chapter{Function-scoped defer}
\label{ch:29}
\index{Function-scoped defer}
\index{Cleanup!defer}

\OnxCodeTerm{defer call;} snapshots the callable and its arguments at registration. An admitted function drains its deferred calls in last-in, first-out order on its normal return or on a propagated Result error. Leaving a loop body does not drain the surrounding function's defer stack. Defers have bounded registration and capture storage. Fatal traps do not run a general unwinder.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
fn main()
{
  var count = 1;
  defer println(count);
  count = 2;
  defer println(count);
  println(3);
}
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

The ordinary call prints 3. The two saved calls print 2 and 1.

\section{Observe registration and drain separately}
Register a deferred call with the current integer counter, update the counter, then register a second call. The deferred observer should see the later registration first and the earlier snapshot second. Repeat the case with an early \OnxCodeTerm{?} error so both paths exercise the drain. Keep a value result independent of the deferred side effects and check it at the caller. When a result owns a managed payload, the emitter protects the pending return until it has run defers and lexical cleanup. The runtime test checks this boundary separately from source spelling.

